{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01FHQYWcgcVM1wVqpkCKjqbJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Pg4RySjS9M2c7PLbe42DjQ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","offset":380,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2346,"cache_read_input_tokens":86565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2346},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-fcfa8dd3d055","uuid":"c0d8bfeb-5d20-4fbc-ab76-92c63143d54e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pg4RySjS9M2c7PLbe42DjQ","type":"tool_result","content":"380\t  },\n381\t  {\n382\t    slug: \"how-malaysians-find-jobs-singapore-2026\",\n383\t    title: \"How Malaysians Can Find Jobs in Singapore (2026 Guide)\",\n384\t    excerpt:\n385\t      \"Step-by-step guide for Malaysian job seekers: job portals, work passes, salary expectations, and interview tips for landing a Singapore role.\",\n386\t    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n387\t    category: \"Career & Work\",\n388\t    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n389\t    readTime: \"10 min read\",\n390\t    tags: [\"singapore\", \"jobs\", \"career\", \"work-pass\", \"malaysia\", \"job-search\"],\n391\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/singapore-jobs-guide.jpg\",\n392\t    content: `\n393\t<h2>Why Malaysians Move to Singapore</h2>\n394\t<p>Singapore attracts Malaysian professionals seeking higher salaries. A RM6,000 earner might earn SGD6,500 (RM24,000)—a massive jump. The economy, institutions, and international companies create opportunities unavailable in Malaysia. Securing a role requires strategy, timing, and understanding visa rules.</p>\n395\t\n396\t<h2>Understanding Singapore Work Passes</h2>\n397\t<p>Singapore issues three main work passes for foreigners. <strong>Employment Pass (EP)</strong> is for professionals and managers earning SGD3,600+ monthly with recognised qualifications. <strong>S Pass</strong> targets skilled workers earning SGD2,500–3,600 with technical expertise or credentials or certifications. <strong>Work Permit</strong> is for semi-skilled and unskilled workers in manufacturing, construction, and service sectors.</p>\n398\t<p>Your employer must sponsor your visa—you cannot apply independently. After job offer, your sponsor submits the application to MOM (Ministry of Manpower). Processing takes 2–4 weeks. Once approved, you have 30 days to enter Singapore. Total timeline from application to starting work: typically 4–6 weeks. Plan accordingly. Most Malaysians aim for EP or S Pass.</p>\n399\t\n400\t<h2>Step 1: Choose the Right Job Portals</h2>\n401\t<p>Don't apply blindly everywhere. Malaysians have five proven platforms. Set your LinkedIn profile to indicate visa sponsorship required—recruiters filter by this. Update your profile with your Malaysian location but indicate \"open to relocation to Singapore.\" Many recruiters search specifically for Malaysian talent.</p>\n402\t<ol>\n403\t<li><strong>LinkedIn Singapore:</strong> Filter by location Singapore, set visa sponsorship required. Recruiters actively headhunt here. Direct applications bypass gatekeeping. Tailor each cover note mentioning your interest in Singapore and timeline.</li>\n404\t<li><strong>JobStreet Singapore:</strong> Malaysia's dominant job portal dominates Singapore too. Search \"Work Visa Sponsorship: Yes\" for 5,000+ roles monthly. Save job alerts; new roles appear daily.</li>\n405\t<li><strong>MyCareersFuture:</strong> Singapore's official government-backed portal with 20,000+ live listings. Updated daily, zero spam, zero recruiter nonsense. Most reputable employers post here first.</li>\n406\t<li><strong>Glassdoor Singapore:</strong> Research company culture, salaries, interview experiences, and manager reviews before applying. Read Malaysian employee reviews specifically—they often describe visa sponsorship processes.</li>\n407\t<li><strong>Angel List & TechJobs:</strong> Startups offer better relocation packages and visa certainty than traditional corporates. Smaller companies move faster on hiring too.</li>\n408\t</ol>\n409\t\n410\t<h2>Step 2: Engage Recruitment Agencies</h2>\n411\t<p>Agencies fast-track Malaysia-to-Singapore placements and negotiate salaries. Legitimate agencies never charge you—employer pays commission. Top names: <strong>Michael Page Singapore, Randstad, PageGroup, Morgan McKinley</strong>. Ask: \"How many Malaysians placed in 12 months?\" and \"What's your visa timeline?\" They handle housing, transportation, visa coordination. A good recruiter saves you RM2,000–5,000 in salary gains. Avoid upfront-fee agencies—they're scams.</p>\n412\t\n413\t<h2>Step 3: Know Salary Reality</h2>\n414\t<p><strong>Entry-level (0–2yrs):</strong> SGD3,500–4,500 monthly. <strong>Mid-career (2–5yrs):</strong> SGD5,500–8,500. <strong>Senior (5+yrs):</strong> SGD12,000–20,000+. Living costs: rent SGD1,000–1,500 for shared HDB flats, transport SGD150/month, food SGD500–800. Net savings: SGD2,000–6,000/month after expenses. Always negotiate before accepting—Singapore expects it. Lowball offers set your ceiling for years. Most employers offer SGD500–800 housing allowance. Ask explicitly. Your first Singapore salary is foundational.</p>\n415\t\n416\t<h2>Step 4: Master Interview Execution</h2>\n417\t<p>Singapore interviews follow a 2–3 round structure: HR screening (30 min), technical assessment (60–90 min), senior manager interview (45 min). Dress business formal. Arrive 10 minutes early. Research the company's Singapore operations, not just global operations.</p>\n418\t<p>Address visa sponsorship proactively: \"I understand Singapore's visa timelines and can support documentation immediately.\" This signals professionalism and reduces employer hesitation. Technical interviews are rigorous—prepare live coding, case studies, or design problems. Practice mock interviews with colleagues. Anticipate salary questions and have your range ready. Singapore candidates expect you to negotiate—silence signals weakness.</p>\n419\t\n420\t<h2>Step 5: Get Your Timeline Right</h2>\n421\t<p>Apply 8–12 weeks before your ideal start date. Visa processing is 2–4 weeks post-offer. Your Malaysian employer requires 30 days notice. Timeline: Apply week 1 → Interviews weeks 2–4 → Offer week 4–5 → Give notice week 5 → Visa processing weeks 5–9 → Start Singapore week 12. This buffer prevents conflicts. Some companies are slower; international tech firms move fastest (2–3 week hiring cycles). Local firms take 6–8 weeks. Ask recruiters for their average hiring timeline.</p>\n422\t\n423\t<h2>Accommodation Strategy</h2>\n424\t<p>HDB flats (Housing Development Board—Singapore's public housing) are your best value: SGD1,000–1,500/month for a shared room. Private condominiums cost SGD1,800–3,000. For your first month, use <strong>HeyStay, Airbnb, PropertyGuru</strong> while apartment hunting. Once hired, contact your company's HR for corporate housing programs—many large employers have partner blocks with discounts.</p>\n425\t<p>If job-hunting while based in Malaysia, <a href=\"/rooms\">stay at Pelangi Capsule in Johor Bahru</a>—just 20 minutes from the Causeway—at RM50–80 per night. You can interview in Singapore daily while keeping accommodation costs low.</p>\n426\t\n427\t<h2>Optimize Application Materials</h2>\n428\t<p>Highlight transferable skills: leadership, languages, cross-cultural experience. Singapore values multilingual candidates. Tailor cover letters—mention Singapore specifically, not generic templates. LinkedIn is your digital resume. Get recommendations. Set headline to \"Role Title | Open to Singapore Relocation\". Emphasize any English, Mandarin, or Tamil skills—multinational firms value them.</p>\n429\t\n430\t<h2>Common Mistakes Malaysian Job Seekers Make</h2>\n431\t<ul>\n432\t<li>Applying to roles requiring 3+ years experience when you have 1.5. Auto-rejection before visa sponsorship is even considered.</li>\n433\t<li>Falsifying qualifications or experience. Singapore employers verify credentials rigorously with universities and past employers. Visa rejection follows immediately.</li>\n434\t<li>Ignoring Glassdoor reviews. Visa sponsorship doesn't guarantee a healthy workplace. Bad companies exist everywhere.</li>\n435\t<li>Renegotiating salary after receiving a verbal offer. Once visa processing initiates, you've lost all leverage. Lock salary before saying yes.</li>\n436\t<li>Ignoring relocation costs. Factor SGD2,000–3,000 for deposits, airfare, and setup. Some sponsors offer relocation allowances—ask.</li>\n437\t</ul>\n438\t\n439\t<h2>Resources & Support</h2>\n440\t<p><strong>Ministry of Manpower (MOM):</strong> mom.gov.sg—official visa requirements, forms, processing status. Read Employment Pass and S Pass guides thoroughly. <strong>Expats in Singapore communities:</strong> Reddit's r/singapore and Facebook groups have active Malaysians answering relocation questions daily.</p>\n441\t\n442\t<h2>Making Your Move</h2>\n443\t<p>Cross-border job hunting is achievable with clear strategy. Start applications 10 weeks ahead. Use LinkedIn and MyCareersFuture daily. Engage reputable agencies. Understand visa timelines and requirements. The salary premium is substantial—expect 3–5x growth. The process is bureaucratic but predictable. Many Malaysians hesitate due to visa complexity; educated candidates succeed. Move intentionally, and you'll cross the Causeway as a prepared professional ready to thrive in Singapore's economy.</p>\n444\t`,\n445\t  },\n446\t  {\n447\t    slug: \"singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians\",\n448\t    title: \"Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians: EP vs S Pass vs Work Permit\",\n449\t    excerpt:\n450\t      \"Understand the three main Singapore work pass types for Malaysians: Employment Pass, S Pass, and Work Permit. Compare salary minimums, eligibility, and which visa suits your career stage.\",\n451\t    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n452\t    category: \"Career & Work\",\n453\t    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n454\t    readTime: \"12 min read\",\n455\t    tags: [\"singapore\", \"work-pass\", \"career\", \"expat\", \"malaysia\", \"employment-pass\", \"visa\"],\n456\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/singapore-work-pass-guide.jpg\",\n457\t    content: `\n458\t<h2>Singapore's Work Visa System Explained</h2>\n459\t<p>Malaysia is Singapore's top source of migrant workers, accounting for 30% of foreign labour. The Ministry of Manpower (MOM) manages work passes under three main categories: Employment Pass (EP), S Pass, and Work Permit. Each serves a different skill level and salary band. Understanding which pass you qualify for is critical before job hunting.</p>\n460\t\n461\t<h2>Quick Comparison: EP vs S Pass vs Work Permit</h2>\n462\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:16px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n463\t<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\">\n464\t<th><strong>Feature</strong></th>\n465\t<th><strong>Employment Pass (EP)</strong></th>\n466\t<th><strong>S Pass</strong></th>\n467\t<th><strong>Work Permit</strong></th>\n468\t</tr>\n469\t<tr>\n470\t<td><strong>Minimum Salary</strong></td>\n471\t<td>SGD 5,000/month</td>\n472\t<td>SGD 3,150/month</td>\n473\t<td>No fixed minimum</td>\n474\t</tr>\n475\t<tr>\n476\t<td><strong>Target Profile</strong></td>\n477\t<td>Degree-holders, senior roles</td>\n478\t<td>Diploma or 5yrs experience</td>\n479\t<td>Semi-skilled, unskilled labour</td>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-fcfa8dd3d055","uuid":"8e4c233e-e62e-40be-88a2-d3e782320931","timestamp":"2026-04-13T20:49:05.292Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"  },\n  {\n    slug: \"how-malaysians-find-jobs-singapore-2026\",\n    title: \"How Malaysians Can Find Jobs in Singapore (2026 Guide)\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"Step-by-step guide for Malaysian job seekers: job portals, work passes, salary expectations, and interview tips for landing a Singapore role.\",\n    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n    category: \"Career & Work\",\n    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n    readTime: \"10 min read\",\n    tags: [\"singapore\", \"jobs\", \"career\", \"work-pass\", \"malaysia\", \"job-search\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/singapore-jobs-guide.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Why Malaysians Move to Singapore</h2>\n<p>Singapore attracts Malaysian professionals seeking higher salaries. A RM6,000 earner might earn SGD6,500 (RM24,000)—a massive jump. The economy, institutions, and international companies create opportunities unavailable in Malaysia. Securing a role requires strategy, timing, and understanding visa rules.</p>\n\n<h2>Understanding Singapore Work Passes</h2>\n<p>Singapore issues three main work passes for foreigners. <strong>Employment Pass (EP)</strong> is for professionals and managers earning SGD3,600+ monthly with recognised qualifications. <strong>S Pass</strong> targets skilled workers earning SGD2,500–3,600 with technical expertise or credentials or certifications. <strong>Work Permit</strong> is for semi-skilled and unskilled workers in manufacturing, construction, and service sectors.</p>\n<p>Your employer must sponsor your visa—you cannot apply independently. After job offer, your sponsor submits the application to MOM (Ministry of Manpower). Processing takes 2–4 weeks. Once approved, you have 30 days to enter Singapore. Total timeline from application to starting work: typically 4–6 weeks. Plan accordingly. Most Malaysians aim for EP or S Pass.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 1: Choose the Right Job Portals</h2>\n<p>Don't apply blindly everywhere. Malaysians have five proven platforms. Set your LinkedIn profile to indicate visa sponsorship required—recruiters filter by this. Update your profile with your Malaysian location but indicate \"open to relocation to Singapore.\" Many recruiters search specifically for Malaysian talent.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>LinkedIn Singapore:</strong> Filter by location Singapore, set visa sponsorship required. Recruiters actively headhunt here. Direct applications bypass gatekeeping. Tailor each cover note mentioning your interest in Singapore and timeline.</li>\n<li><strong>JobStreet Singapore:</strong> Malaysia's dominant job portal dominates Singapore too. Search \"Work Visa Sponsorship: Yes\" for 5,000+ roles monthly. Save job alerts; new roles appear daily.</li>\n<li><strong>MyCareersFuture:</strong> Singapore's official government-backed portal with 20,000+ live listings. Updated daily, zero spam, zero recruiter nonsense. Most reputable employers post here first.</li>\n<li><strong>Glassdoor Singapore:</strong> Research company culture, salaries, interview experiences, and manager reviews before applying. Read Malaysian employee reviews specifically—they often describe visa sponsorship processes.</li>\n<li><strong>Angel List & TechJobs:</strong> Startups offer better relocation packages and visa certainty than traditional corporates. Smaller companies move faster on hiring too.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>Step 2: Engage Recruitment Agencies</h2>\n<p>Agencies fast-track Malaysia-to-Singapore placements and negotiate salaries. Legitimate agencies never charge you—employer pays commission. Top names: <strong>Michael Page Singapore, Randstad, PageGroup, Morgan McKinley</strong>. Ask: \"How many Malaysians placed in 12 months?\" and \"What's your visa timeline?\" They handle housing, transportation, visa coordination. A good recruiter saves you RM2,000–5,000 in salary gains. Avoid upfront-fee agencies—they're scams.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 3: Know Salary Reality</h2>\n<p><strong>Entry-level (0–2yrs):</strong> SGD3,500–4,500 monthly. <strong>Mid-career (2–5yrs):</strong> SGD5,500–8,500. <strong>Senior (5+yrs):</strong> SGD12,000–20,000+. Living costs: rent SGD1,000–1,500 for shared HDB flats, transport SGD150/month, food SGD500–800. Net savings: SGD2,000–6,000/month after expenses. Always negotiate before accepting—Singapore expects it. Lowball offers set your ceiling for years. Most employers offer SGD500–800 housing allowance. Ask explicitly. Your first Singapore salary is foundational.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 4: Master Interview Execution</h2>\n<p>Singapore interviews follow a 2–3 round structure: HR screening (30 min), technical assessment (60–90 min), senior manager interview (45 min). Dress business formal. Arrive 10 minutes early. Research the company's Singapore operations, not just global operations.</p>\n<p>Address visa sponsorship proactively: \"I understand Singapore's visa timelines and can support documentation immediately.\" This signals professionalism and reduces employer hesitation. Technical interviews are rigorous—prepare live coding, case studies, or design problems. Practice mock interviews with colleagues. Anticipate salary questions and have your range ready. Singapore candidates expect you to negotiate—silence signals weakness.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 5: Get Your Timeline Right</h2>\n<p>Apply 8–12 weeks before your ideal start date. Visa processing is 2–4 weeks post-offer. Your Malaysian employer requires 30 days notice. Timeline: Apply week 1 → Interviews weeks 2–4 → Offer week 4–5 → Give notice week 5 → Visa processing weeks 5–9 → Start Singapore week 12. This buffer prevents conflicts. Some companies are slower; international tech firms move fastest (2–3 week hiring cycles). Local firms take 6–8 weeks. Ask recruiters for their average hiring timeline.</p>\n\n<h2>Accommodation Strategy</h2>\n<p>HDB flats (Housing Development Board—Singapore's public housing) are your best value: SGD1,000–1,500/month for a shared room. Private condominiums cost SGD1,800–3,000. For your first month, use <strong>HeyStay, Airbnb, PropertyGuru</strong> while apartment hunting. Once hired, contact your company's HR for corporate housing programs—many large employers have partner blocks with discounts.</p>\n<p>If job-hunting while based in Malaysia, <a href=\"/rooms\">stay at Pelangi Capsule in Johor Bahru</a>—just 20 minutes from the Causeway—at RM50–80 per night. You can interview in Singapore daily while keeping accommodation costs low.</p>\n\n<h2>Optimize Application Materials</h2>\n<p>Highlight transferable skills: leadership, languages, cross-cultural experience. Singapore values multilingual candidates. Tailor cover letters—mention Singapore specifically, not generic templates. LinkedIn is your digital resume. Get recommendations. Set headline to \"Role Title | Open to Singapore Relocation\". Emphasize any English, Mandarin, or Tamil skills—multinational firms value them.</p>\n\n<h2>Common Mistakes Malaysian Job Seekers Make</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Applying to roles requiring 3+ years experience when you have 1.5. Auto-rejection before visa sponsorship is even considered.</li>\n<li>Falsifying qualifications or experience. Singapore employers verify credentials rigorously with universities and past employers. Visa rejection follows immediately.</li>\n<li>Ignoring Glassdoor reviews. Visa sponsorship doesn't guarantee a healthy workplace. Bad companies exist everywhere.</li>\n<li>Renegotiating salary after receiving a verbal offer. Once visa processing initiates, you've lost all leverage. Lock salary before saying yes.</li>\n<li>Ignoring relocation costs. Factor SGD2,000–3,000 for deposits, airfare, and setup. Some sponsors offer relocation allowances—ask.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Resources & Support</h2>\n<p><strong>Ministry of Manpower (MOM):</strong> mom.gov.sg—official visa requirements, forms, processing status. Read Employment Pass and S Pass guides thoroughly. <strong>Expats in Singapore communities:</strong> Reddit's r/singapore and Facebook groups have active Malaysians answering relocation questions daily.</p>\n\n<h2>Making Your Move</h2>\n<p>Cross-border job hunting is achievable with clear strategy. Start applications 10 weeks ahead. Use LinkedIn and MyCareersFuture daily. Engage reputable agencies. Understand visa timelines and requirements. The salary premium is substantial—expect 3–5x growth. The process is bureaucratic but predictable. Many Malaysians hesitate due to visa complexity; educated candidates succeed. Move intentionally, and you'll cross the Causeway as a prepared professional ready to thrive in Singapore's economy.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians\",\n    title: \"Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians: EP vs S Pass vs Work Permit\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"Understand the three main Singapore work pass types for Malaysians: Employment Pass, S Pass, and Work Permit. Compare salary minimums, eligibility, and which visa suits your career stage.\",\n    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n    category: \"Career & Work\",\n    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n    readTime: \"12 min read\",\n    tags: [\"singapore\", \"work-pass\", \"career\", \"expat\", \"malaysia\", \"employment-pass\", \"visa\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/singapore-work-pass-guide.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Singapore's Work Visa System Explained</h2>\n<p>Malaysia is Singapore's top source of migrant workers, accounting for 30% of foreign labour. The Ministry of Manpower (MOM) manages work passes under three main categories: Employment Pass (EP), S Pass, and Work Permit. Each serves a different skill level and salary band. Understanding which pass you qualify for is critical before job hunting.</p>\n\n<h2>Quick Comparison: EP vs S Pass vs Work Permit</h2>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:16px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\">\n<th><strong>Feature</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Employment Pass (EP)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>S Pass</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Work Permit</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Minimum Salary</strong></td>\n<td>SGD 5,000/month</td>\n<td>SGD 3,150/month</td>\n<td>No fixed minimum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Target Profile</strong></td>\n<td>Degree-holders, senior roles</td>\n<td>Diploma or 5yrs experience</td>\n<td>Semi-skilled, unskilled labour</td>","numLines":100,"startLine":380,"totalLines":700}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0173Uo1xMBLeY1inW6HyqtMw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! I can see the pattern. Now let me look further to find the most recent posts (US-243, US-244) to see them:","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3236,"cache_read_input_tokens":88911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3236},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-fcfa8dd3d055","uuid":"f78e4eba-bf60-48ea-8f87-ad92a021f66d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0173Uo1xMBLeY1inW6HyqtMw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WoJ2znwFhKLD9u4wsAvPap","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","offset":500,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3236,"cache_read_input_tokens":88911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3236},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-fcfa8dd3d055","uuid":"150462b3-40d2-44bb-9082-d1f3410ac8a6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WoJ2znwFhKLD9u4wsAvPap","type":"tool_result","content":"500\t<td><strong>Sector Restrictions</strong></td>\n501\t<td>All sectors</td>\n502\t<td>Most sectors (some restricted)</td>\n503\t<td>Only specified sectors</td>\n504\t</tr>\n505\t</table>\n506\t\n507\t<h2>Employment Pass (EP): For Professionals</h2>\n508\t<p><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> Degree-holders, managers, specialists. Minimum salary SGD5,000/month. No work experience requirement, but higher salary expectation if experienced.</p>\n509\t<p><strong>Employer responsibilities:</strong> Employer files with MOM using standard form. They bear sponsorship costs (typically SGD500–1,500). Processing takes 1–4 weeks. No quota limits; approved applications are processed first-in-first-out.</p>\n510\t<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Fresh graduates from prestigious universities, tech professionals, finance roles, engineering, and management positions. If you have a degree, aim for EP—it's the most stable and flexible pass.</p>\n511\t\n512\t<h2>S Pass: The Middle Ground</h2>\n513\t<p><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> Diploma holders or those with 5+ years relevant work experience. Minimum salary SGD3,150/month. Evaluated on skill level, proven experience, and salary competitiveness.</p>\n514\t<p><strong>Processing:</strong> 2–8 weeks, slower than EP because MOM evaluates local job market impact. There are annual quotas; applications processed when quota available. This pass is popular with mid-career Malaysians.</p>\n515\t<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Technicians, supervisors, mid-level specialists without university degrees. If you have strong experience but no degree, S Pass is realistic and widely available.</p>\n516\t\n517\t<h2>Work Permit: For Specific Sectors</h2>\n518\t<p><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> Semi-skilled and unskilled workers in approved sectors: construction, marine, food, healthcare, domestic work. No degree required; employers must show they cannot fill role locally.</p>\n519\t<p><strong>Restrictions:</strong> Highly sector-specific. Work Permits are not portable across employers. Processing is fastest (1–2 weeks) but quotas vary by sector and are tightly managed.</p>\n520\t<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Hospitality workers, construction labourers, healthcare aides, cleaners. If you lack formal qualifications, Work Permit is the entry point.</p>\n521\t\n522\t<h2>FAQ: Your Singapore Work Pass Questions Answered</h2>\n523\t\n524\t<h3>Q: I have a diploma but 8 years experience. Can I get an EP?</h3>\n525\t<p><strong>A:</strong> Unlikely. MOM policy requires degree for EP unless you're a rare specialist (e.g., executive chef with global recognition). With 8 years experience, your S Pass is stronger. Some borderline cases where MOM approves EP anyway—always try, worst case is rejection. Your employer can reapply as S Pass.</p>\n526\t\n527\t<h3>Q: What salary should I negotiate for?</h3>\n528\t<p><strong>A:</strong> EP minimum is SGD5,000; most employers offer SGD5,500–8,000 for mid-career (3–5 years). S Pass starts at SGD3,150 but realistic offers are SGD3,800–5,500. Never go below the MOM minimum—visa will be rejected. Always negotiate above minimum; accepting SGD5,000 EP sets your ceiling for 3 years until next renewal.</p>\n529\t\n530\t<h3>Q: Can I bring my family on a Work Permit?</h3>\n531\t<p><strong>A:</strong> No. Work Permits are non-family passes. EP allows spouse + dependent children (usually up to age 22). S Pass dependents require separate approval, rarely granted. If family is priority, aim for EP. If you're Work Permit, your family stays in Malaysia.</p>\n532\t\n533\t<h3>Q: How long does visa processing actually take?</h3>\n534\t<p><strong>A:</strong> MOM publishes \"1–4 weeks\" for EP but reality varies. Straightforward cases (degree verified, salary clear, employer reputable) process in 7–10 days. Complicated cases (foreign degree, education verification delays, conflicting documents) take 3–4 weeks. S Pass slower: 2–8 weeks. Always assume 4 weeks and plan accordingly. Ask your employer's HR for their average—top companies have precedent and process faster.</p>\n535\t\n536\t<h3>Q: Can I change jobs mid-pass?</h3>\n537\t<p><strong>A:</strong> EP: Yes, new employer files for transfer. Salary can drop but not below MOM minimum. Entire transfer takes 1–2 weeks. S Pass: Yes, with same restrictions. Work Permit: No—strictly employer-locked. Changing jobs on Work Permit requires new application, often rejected if different sector.</p>\n538\t\n539\t<h2>Red Flags to Avoid</h2>\n540\t<ul>\n541\t<li><strong>Underpaying offers:</strong> \"We'll sponsor at SGD3,500 for EP.\" Reject immediately—MOM will reject it anyway, wasting 3 weeks.</li>\n542\t<li><strong>Vague timelines:</strong> \"Processing usually takes 2 weeks.\" Push back—get written commitment on timeline.</li>\n543\t<li><strong>Company never sponsored Malaysians:</strong> High rejection risk. Ask HR for past sponsorship success rate. First-time sponsors have slower processing.</li>\n544\t</ul>\n545\t\n546\t<h2>Your Next Move</h2>\n547\t<p>Identify your profile (degree? experience? target salary?) and apply strategically. EP is fastest and most flexible; push for it if degree-eligible. S Pass is realistic for experienced non-graduates. Start applications 12 weeks before desired start date. The Singapore salary premium (3–5x your Malaysia salary) justifies the bureaucracy. Move intentionally, understand your visa category, and cross the Causeway ready to thrive.</p>\n548\t`,\n549\t  },\n550\t  {\n551\t    slug: \"jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026\",\n552\t    title: \"Living in Johor Bahru, Working in Singapore: The Commuter's Guide 2026\",\n553\t    excerpt:\n554\t      \"Cross the Causeway daily and save money. Everything Malaysians need to know about commuting from JB to Singapore: bus options, CIQ crossings, costs, and why it works.\",\n555\t    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n556\t    category: \"Career & Work\",\n557\t    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n558\t    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n559\t    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"singapore\", \"commuter\", \"work\", \"malaysia\", \"cross-border\", \"cost-of-living\"],\n560\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-singapore-commuter.jpg\",\n561\t    content: `\n562\t<h2>The Commuter Advantage</h2>\n563\t<p>Thousands of Malaysians cross the Causeway every weekday to work in Singapore. Why? A Singapore salary (SGD4,000–8,000/month) combined with Johor Bahru living costs (RM2,500–3,500/month for rent, food, transport) creates a savings rate impossible in either country alone. For Malaysian professionals earning SGD5,000 as an <a href=\"singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians\">Employment Pass holder</a>, living in JB while working in Singapore means saving SGD2,000–3,000 monthly.</p>\n564\t<p>This guide covers everything: bus options, CIQ checkpoint crossings, monthly budgets, peak-hour strategies, and why <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> works as a base while you settle in.</p>\n565\t\n566\t<h2>Getting Across: Your Bus Options</h2>\n567\t<p>Five daily shuttle services cross the Causeway. Most commuters use two: <strong>Causeway Link</strong> and <strong>SBS Transit</strong>. Both operate modern, air-conditioned coaches with WiFi.</p>\n568\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:16px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n569\t<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\">\n570\t<th><strong>Service</strong></th>\n571\t<th><strong>Route</strong></th>\n572\t<th><strong>Price (single)</strong></th>\n573\t<th><strong>Duration</strong></th>\n574\t<th><strong>Frequency</strong></th>\n575\t</tr>\n576\t<tr>\n577\t<td><strong>Causeway Link (CW)</strong></td>\n578\t<td>JB Sentral → Singapore (multiple endpoints)</td>\n579\t<td>RM5–6 / SGD2–2.50</td>\n580\t<td>40–60 min</td>\n581\t<td>Every 5–15 min</td>\n582\t</tr>\n583\t<tr>\n584\t<td><strong>SBS Transit</strong></td>\n585\t<td>Larkin Sentral → Singapore</td>\n586\t<td>RM3–4 / SGD1.50</td>\n587\t<td>45–75 min</td>\n588\t<td>Every 10–20 min</td>\n589\t</tr>\n590\t<tr>\n591\t<td><strong>Sunrays Express</strong></td>\n592\t<td>JB CIQ → Woodlands</td>\n593\t<td>RM5 / SGD2</td>\n594\t<td>50–70 min</td>\n595\t<td>Hourly</td>\n596\t</tr>\n597\t<tr>\n598\t<td><strong>Premium (Intercity)</strong></td>\n599\t<td>JB → Singapore downtown</td>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-fcfa8dd3d055","uuid":"5ccf4bff-fa21-4768-9016-6824c7d3ce2f","timestamp":"2026-04-13T20:49:08.045Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"<td><strong>Sector Restrictions</strong></td>\n<td>All sectors</td>\n<td>Most sectors (some restricted)</td>\n<td>Only specified sectors</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Employment Pass (EP): For Professionals</h2>\n<p><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> Degree-holders, managers, specialists. Minimum salary SGD5,000/month. No work experience requirement, but higher salary expectation if experienced.</p>\n<p><strong>Employer responsibilities:</strong> Employer files with MOM using standard form. They bear sponsorship costs (typically SGD500–1,500). Processing takes 1–4 weeks. No quota limits; approved applications are processed first-in-first-out.</p>\n<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Fresh graduates from prestigious universities, tech professionals, finance roles, engineering, and management positions. If you have a degree, aim for EP—it's the most stable and flexible pass.</p>\n\n<h2>S Pass: The Middle Ground</h2>\n<p><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> Diploma holders or those with 5+ years relevant work experience. Minimum salary SGD3,150/month. Evaluated on skill level, proven experience, and salary competitiveness.</p>\n<p><strong>Processing:</strong> 2–8 weeks, slower than EP because MOM evaluates local job market impact. There are annual quotas; applications processed when quota available. This pass is popular with mid-career Malaysians.</p>\n<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Technicians, supervisors, mid-level specialists without university degrees. If you have strong experience but no degree, S Pass is realistic and widely available.</p>\n\n<h2>Work Permit: For Specific Sectors</h2>\n<p><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> Semi-skilled and unskilled workers in approved sectors: construction, marine, food, healthcare, domestic work. No degree required; employers must show they cannot fill role locally.</p>\n<p><strong>Restrictions:</strong> Highly sector-specific. Work Permits are not portable across employers. Processing is fastest (1–2 weeks) but quotas vary by sector and are tightly managed.</p>\n<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Hospitality workers, construction labourers, healthcare aides, cleaners. If you lack formal qualifications, Work Permit is the entry point.</p>\n\n<h2>FAQ: Your Singapore Work Pass Questions Answered</h2>\n\n<h3>Q: I have a diploma but 8 years experience. Can I get an EP?</h3>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> Unlikely. MOM policy requires degree for EP unless you're a rare specialist (e.g., executive chef with global recognition). With 8 years experience, your S Pass is stronger. Some borderline cases where MOM approves EP anyway—always try, worst case is rejection. Your employer can reapply as S Pass.</p>\n\n<h3>Q: What salary should I negotiate for?</h3>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> EP minimum is SGD5,000; most employers offer SGD5,500–8,000 for mid-career (3–5 years). S Pass starts at SGD3,150 but realistic offers are SGD3,800–5,500. Never go below the MOM minimum—visa will be rejected. Always negotiate above minimum; accepting SGD5,000 EP sets your ceiling for 3 years until next renewal.</p>\n\n<h3>Q: Can I bring my family on a Work Permit?</h3>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> No. Work Permits are non-family passes. EP allows spouse + dependent children (usually up to age 22). S Pass dependents require separate approval, rarely granted. If family is priority, aim for EP. If you're Work Permit, your family stays in Malaysia.</p>\n\n<h3>Q: How long does visa processing actually take?</h3>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> MOM publishes \"1–4 weeks\" for EP but reality varies. Straightforward cases (degree verified, salary clear, employer reputable) process in 7–10 days. Complicated cases (foreign degree, education verification delays, conflicting documents) take 3–4 weeks. S Pass slower: 2–8 weeks. Always assume 4 weeks and plan accordingly. Ask your employer's HR for their average—top companies have precedent and process faster.</p>\n\n<h3>Q: Can I change jobs mid-pass?</h3>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> EP: Yes, new employer files for transfer. Salary can drop but not below MOM minimum. Entire transfer takes 1–2 weeks. S Pass: Yes, with same restrictions. Work Permit: No—strictly employer-locked. Changing jobs on Work Permit requires new application, often rejected if different sector.</p>\n\n<h2>Red Flags to Avoid</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Underpaying offers:</strong> \"We'll sponsor at SGD3,500 for EP.\" Reject immediately—MOM will reject it anyway, wasting 3 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Vague timelines:</strong> \"Processing usually takes 2 weeks.\" Push back—get written commitment on timeline.</li>\n<li><strong>Company never sponsored Malaysians:</strong> High rejection risk. Ask HR for past sponsorship success rate. First-time sponsors have slower processing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Your Next Move</h2>\n<p>Identify your profile (degree? experience? target salary?) and apply strategically. EP is fastest and most flexible; push for it if degree-eligible. S Pass is realistic for experienced non-graduates. Start applications 12 weeks before desired start date. The Singapore salary premium (3–5x your Malaysia salary) justifies the bureaucracy. Move intentionally, understand your visa category, and cross the Causeway ready to thrive.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026\",\n    title: \"Living in Johor Bahru, Working in Singapore: The Commuter's Guide 2026\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"Cross the Causeway daily and save money. Everything Malaysians need to know about commuting from JB to Singapore: bus options, CIQ crossings, costs, and why it works.\",\n    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n    category: \"Career & Work\",\n    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"singapore\", \"commuter\", \"work\", \"malaysia\", \"cross-border\", \"cost-of-living\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-singapore-commuter.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>The Commuter Advantage</h2>\n<p>Thousands of Malaysians cross the Causeway every weekday to work in Singapore. Why? A Singapore salary (SGD4,000–8,000/month) combined with Johor Bahru living costs (RM2,500–3,500/month for rent, food, transport) creates a savings rate impossible in either country alone. For Malaysian professionals earning SGD5,000 as an <a href=\"singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians\">Employment Pass holder</a>, living in JB while working in Singapore means saving SGD2,000–3,000 monthly.</p>\n<p>This guide covers everything: bus options, CIQ checkpoint crossings, monthly budgets, peak-hour strategies, and why <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> works as a base while you settle in.</p>\n\n<h2>Getting Across: Your Bus Options</h2>\n<p>Five daily shuttle services cross the Causeway. Most commuters use two: <strong>Causeway Link</strong> and <strong>SBS Transit</strong>. Both operate modern, air-conditioned coaches with WiFi.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:16px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\">\n<th><strong>Service</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Route</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Price (single)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Duration</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Frequency</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Causeway Link (CW)</strong></td>\n<td>JB Sentral → Singapore (multiple endpoints)</td>\n<td>RM5–6 / SGD2–2.50</td>\n<td>40–60 min</td>\n<td>Every 5–15 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SBS Transit</strong></td>\n<td>Larkin Sentral → Singapore</td>\n<td>RM3–4 / SGD1.50</td>\n<td>45–75 min</td>\n<td>Every 10–20 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sunrays Express</strong></td>\n<td>JB CIQ → Woodlands</td>\n<td>RM5 / SGD2</td>\n<td>50–70 min</td>\n<td>Hourly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Premium (Intercity)</strong></td>\n<td>JB → Singapore downtown</td>","numLines":100,"startLine":500,"totalLines":700}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01RbxwvzczKQUj8HeKGxTTNS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! Now I have a good understanding of the structure. The most recent posts are US-243 (singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians) and US-244 (jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026), both dated 2026-04-14. Now I need to add US-247 (foodpanda-delivery-rider-malaysia-guide) before the closing bracket and after the jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026 post.\n\nLet me now plan the implementation:\n\n## Current State\n- File: `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts` contains an array of blog posts\n- Each post has: slug, title, excerpt, date, category, author, readTime, tags, featuredImage, content (HTML)\n- The file ends with rawPosts array closing, then mapping to blogPosts with readingTimeMinutes computed\n- Previous similar stories (US-243, US-244) were completed successfully\n\n## Problem Identified\nNeed to add a new blog post for US-247: \"How to Register as a Foodpanda Delivery Rider in Malaysia (2026)\"\n\n## Planned Changes\n1. Add a new blog post object to the rawPosts array in `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`\n   - slug: 'foodpanda-delivery-rider-malaysia-guide'\n   - title: 'How to Register as a Foodpanda Delivery Rider in Malaysia (2026)'\n   - excerpt: ~80 chars about temp income while waiting for Singapore work pass\n   - date: '2026-04-14'\n   - category: 'Career & Work'\n   - author: 'Pelangi Team'\n   - readTime: '6 min read'\n   - tags: relevant tags (foodpanda, delivery, gig-economy, malaysia, work)\n   - featuredImage: appropriate image\n   - content: HTML with numbered registration steps, vehicle requirements, earnings RM20-50/hour\n   - Keep HTML under 6000 characters\n\nI'll need to verify the build passes with npm run build, ensure TypeScript compiles without errors, and check for any linting issues. For the content itself, I'm structuring it with: introduction on why this works as temporary income, enumerated vehicle requirements, numbered registration steps, earnings breakdown showing RM20-50/hour potential, practical success tips, and a CTA linking to /book.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2760,"cache_read_input_tokens":92147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2760},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-fcfa8dd3d055","uuid":"a8318350-301d-46a7-b26b-4ff2230baf60"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01RbxwvzczKQUj8HeKGxTTNS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll create a diagnosis block and implement US-247:\n\n## Current State\n- File: `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts` contains a TypeScript array of blog post objects\n- Each post follows the same interface: slug, title, excerpt, date, category, author, readTime, tags, featuredImage, content (HTML)\n- Recent posts (US-243, US-244) follow the exact same pattern with HTML content under 6000 characters\n- The rawPosts array ends at line 689, followed by the export statement\n\n## Problem Identified\nNeed to add a new blog post for US-247: \"How to Register as a Foodpanda Delivery Rider in Malaysia (2026)\"\n- Slug: 'foodpanda-delivery-rider-malaysia-guide'\n- Category: 'Career & Work'\n- Date: '2026-04-14'\n- ReadTime: '6 min read'\n- Must include: numbered registration steps, vehicle requirements, earnings estimate (RM20-50/hour)\n- HTML content must be under 6000 characters\n\n## Planned Changes\n1. Add a new blog post object to the rawPosts array in `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts` (before the closing bracket at line 689)\n2. Include all required fields matching the structure of previous posts\n3. Write content with:\n   - Introduction explaining gig work opportunity\n   - Vehicle requirements (list format)\n   - Numbered registration steps (5-7 steps)\n   - Earnings breakdown section\n   - Tips for success\n   - CTA to /book\n4. Ensure content stays under 6000 characters\n5. Run npm run build to verify"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2760,"cache_read_input_tokens":92147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2760},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7a92fd6-4b90-4f55-947c-